00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.460
and explain the kind
of action you can take

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:19.380
when you're working an
insider risk management case.

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.600
Insider risk
management is designed

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:25.260
to help you address the
type of risk and violations

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:28.770
that can come from
within the organization.

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:33.150
That can include intentional
misuse, like data theft,

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.400
or unintentional actions,

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.080
like accidental leaks
or policy violations.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:42.180
The value here is correlation.

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:46.020
Instead of looking one
isolated incident,

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:49.380
insider risk management
brings signals together

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:54.710
to highlight patterns that
deserve investigation.

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:58.970
Here we walk through the
workflow used by Microsoft 365,

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:02.840
to identify and resolve
internal risk activities

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:06.870
and compliance issues with
insider risk management.

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:11.700
The workflow focuses on moving
from signals to indicator

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:18.150
into policy detection, then
into triage and investigation,

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.330
and finally, into taking
appropriate action

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:24.960
to resolve the risk while
aligning to privacy

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:28.830
and compliance expectations.

00:10:28.830 --> 00:10:31.080
Managing insider
risk policies starts

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.900
with choosing the
right policy template.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:37.710
Common templates include
departing employees data theft,

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:42.270
data leaks, and currently
preview security policy

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:47.760
violations, health record
misuse, and risky browser usage.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.450
Once you select a template, you
can configure policy settings

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:53.850
such as privacy indicators,

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:57.330
the timeframe the
policy should evaluate,

00:10:57.330 --> 00:11:03.180
and the intelligent detection
used to identify risky behavior

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:08.400
with the right balance of
visibility and privacy.

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:12.780
Now let's turn to eDiscovery,
specifically how to search

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:15.600
for content using
Microsoft Purview.

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:20.460
This capability is critical when
an investigation requires you

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:24.810
to find, preserve, and
potentially export content

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:30.210
across Microsoft 365 services in
a way that supports compliant

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:33.460
and legal defensibility.

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:37.860
In this module, the learning
objectives are straightforward.

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:40.980
You'll learn how to assign the
role and permission required

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:45.450
to access Microsoft Purview
eDiscovery, how to create

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:49.830
and manage keys used to
run eDiscovery searches,

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:54.330
how to define search scope and
build queries using conditions,

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:58.890
keywords, and Copilot-generated
prompts, and finally,

00:11:58.890 --> 00:12:03.420
how to run searches and validate
results using statistics

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:05.710
or random samples.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:09.060
Let's define what eDiscovery is

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:12.660
and how it compares
to Content Search.

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:16.770
eDiscovery is a tool in the
Microsoft Purview portal

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:19.230
that lets you
search for content,

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:22.530
place hold, and export content.

00:12:22.530 --> 00:12:26.820
Access is limited to users who
have assigned the right role,

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:31.410
typically eDiscovery managers
or eDiscovery administrators

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:35.490
and licensing impacts
which feature you can use

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:41.310
with Core eDiscovery
included in E3 and E5.

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:44.340
You'll use eDiscovery for
internal investigation

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:50.220
like HR cases, or suspected
data misuse, as well as legal

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:54.570
and regulatory requests, data
subject rights requests,

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:58.470
and incident responses
or breach reviews.

00:12:58.470 --> 00:13:01.890
The big idea is that
eDiscovery helps security

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:04.950
and compliance teams
find, preserve,

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:10.050
and export content during
investigations, legal inquiries,

00:13:10.050 --> 00:13:12.930
or incident responses.

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:16.230
It's also important to
use eDiscovery as more

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:18.600
than a simple search feature.

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:22.560
While Content Search can
retrieve data quickly,

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:28.020
it doesn't provide the same
auditability and access control

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.810
that eDiscovery provides.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:34.950
eDiscovery, on the other
hand, is designed for secure,

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:39.090
auditable searches
tied to a formal case

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:41.670
with role-based permission.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:43.350
On to licensing side.

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:48.150
Core eDiscovery is available in
E5, and advanced eDiscovery,

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:51.720
often referred to as
"Premium," adds capability

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:55.800
like custodian holds,
legal hold notifications,

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:57.960
and built-in review sets.

00:13:57.960 --> 00:14:01.020
Even if you're not
using the premium tier,

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:04.710
understanding the difference
helps you assess what your

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:06.750
environment can support.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:10.650
A helpful way to internalize
this is to think

00:14:10.650 --> 00:14:12.990
through a real-world
investigation,

00:14:12.990 --> 00:14:17.220
like a phishing attack
or an insider leak,

00:14:17.220 --> 00:14:20.250
where you create a
case, run a search,

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:23.370
and export results for review.

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:26.340
Before you can use
eDiscovery access,

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:29.280
you have to explicitly assign

00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:34.350
to ensure investigation
remains secure and auditable.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:38.160
The required roles are
typically eDiscovery managers

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:42.840
who can create and manage
cases, search and export,

00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:44.970
and eDiscovery administrators

00:14:44.970 --> 00:14:49.410
who have all the manager
permissions plus the ability

00:14:49.410 --> 00:14:53.370
to manage role assignments
and control settings.

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:57.240
To assign these roles, you go to
the Microsoft Purview portal,

00:14:57.240 --> 00:15:01.800
then you navigate to
"Settings," "Roles and Scope,"

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:06.270
and "Role Groups," and add users
to the appropriate role group.

00:15:06.270 --> 00:15:08.460
To confirm access,
you can navigate

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:11.190
into eDiscovery in the portal.

00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:14.970
If the user has the
correct role and license,

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:17.850
the "Create Case"
page should appear.

00:15:17.850 --> 00:15:23.640
A key operational point is that
access is scoped intentionally.

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:26.820
Even if someone has
an eDiscovery tool,

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:30.450
they may only see their
case they're a member of,

00:15:30.450 --> 00:15:33.120
which supports least
privileged access

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:37.530
and keeps unrelated
investigation separated.

00:15:37.530 --> 00:15:40.980
This also helps with common
troubleshooting during

00:15:40.980 --> 00:15:42.450
hands-on work.

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:46.050
If someone can't see
anything, it's usually

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:50.010
because they're missing
either the role assignment

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:52.300
or the case membership.

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:57.480
In Microsoft Purview eDiscovery,

00:15:57.480 --> 00:16:01.290
every search is
created within a case.

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:04.620
The case acts as a secure
auditable workspace

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:06.750
for managing investigation.

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:12.150
That requirement matters because
it enforces access control,

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:15.840
keeps a consistent
investigation structure,

00:16:15.840 --> 00:16:20.760
and ensures every action,
searching, accessing, exporting,

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:23.610
is logged in the
context of the case.

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:27.960
Only members of a case can
access it, even if they're

00:16:27.960 --> 00:16:32.460
on an eDiscovery role, and when
somebody else creates the case,

00:16:32.460 --> 00:16:35.850
they're automatically
added as a member.

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:41.610
To create a search, you go to
the eDiscovery open cases,

00:16:41.610 --> 00:16:44.040
and then select "Create Search".

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:47.580
You provide the case name
and the search name,

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:51.360
and in a single step, Purview
creates both the case

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:52.920
and the search.

00:16:52.920 --> 00:16:57.240
This case may structure
is what makes eDiscovery

00:16:57.240 --> 00:16:59.220
legally defensible.

00:16:59.220 --> 00:17:04.650
It provides a container where
you investigate diligence,

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:08.460
and data integrity
can be demonstrated

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:12.640
if the result ever needs
to stand up to scrutiny.

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:17.580
Now, let's walk through
conducting a search.

00:17:17.580 --> 00:17:19.680
The purpose is to locate content

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:24.810
across Microsoft 365
services that's relevant

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:28.740
to a security incident,
a regulatory request,

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:31.650
or an internal investigation.

00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:35.820
You start by defining the
criteria in your search,

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:40.880
set the filter using keyword or
conditions such as date, sender,

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:47.030
or content type, then you select
your data source, user, groups,

00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:51.810
site, or even broader
tenant-wide sources,

00:17:51.810 --> 00:17:53.640
depending on whether you want

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:57.210
to narrow the scope
or cast a wider net.

00:17:57.210 --> 00:17:59.280
Next, you build a query logic.

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:01.290
You can use the
condition builder

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:03.450
if you prefer guided experience,

00:18:03.450 --> 00:18:09.060
write KQL for more advanced
filtering, or where possible,

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:13.350
try Copilot or "Search by
File" in Purview to generate

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:16.340
or accelerate the search logic.

00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:19.820
After you've run the search,
you review settings,

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:23.180
reusing statistics,
how many items match,

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:27.900
or a sample to validate
relevance before you explore.

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:31.650
From there, you refine
and rerun as needed.

00:18:31.650 --> 00:18:35.700
A practical best practice is
to avoid scoping too broadly

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:40.050
at the start, because that
creates review overload.

00:18:40.050 --> 00:18:45.120
Instead, start small,
validate, and then expand only

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:48.460
when the evidence tells you to.

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:51.120
Once you have the right result,

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:56.280
the next step is exporting
them along with metadata.

00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:59.940
For investigation
documentation, legal review,

00:18:59.940 --> 00:19:03.120
or external handoff,
you start an export

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:07.410
by selecting a completed
search of the search step

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:11.870
and choosing what to explore,
such as index items,

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:16.130
partially indexed items, or
both, then you configure option

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:21.390
for workload like OneDrive,
SharePoint, Exchange, Teams.

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:25.230
For example, whether to
include version history,

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:30.030
include threaded messages,
or cloud attachments,

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:35.520
you can chose the output format,
you want to use PST or MSG along

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:39.330
with a folder structure
and friendly naming.

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:42.090
As the export runs,
you track status

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:44.730
and detail in process manager.

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:47.580
When it's ready, you go
to the "Export" tab,

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:50.130
review the export overview,

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:54.210
and download the package
along with reports.

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:57.120
This step matters for
a chain of custody.

00:19:57.120 --> 00:20:03.090
Export packages can include
original format and metadata,

00:20:03.090 --> 00:20:06.750
and they're typically
delivered as secure packages

00:20:06.750 --> 00:20:09.000
with manifest and logs.

00:20:09.000 --> 00:20:14.250
Hashes and export identifiers
help demonstrate integrity,

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:19.050
showing the data hasn't been
modified, while secure,

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:22.890
time-limited download links
helps protect the export

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:24.790
during transfer.

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:32.250
Now we move into Microsoft
Purview Audit, which is all

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:37.230
about understanding activities
across Microsoft 365,

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:40.110
who did what, when, and where.

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:45.710
Audit data becomes critical
evidence for incident response,

00:20:45.710 --> 00:20:50.060
compliance, verification,
and deeper investigation

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:54.650
where you need a
reliable activity trail.

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:58.400
In this module, you'll learn
how to identify the difference

00:20:58.400 --> 00:21:03.350
between Microsoft Purview Audit
Standard and Audit Premium,

00:21:03.350 --> 00:21:05.750
configure Microsoft
Purview Audit

00:21:05.750 --> 00:21:09.950
for optimate log management
and perform audit

00:21:09.950 --> 00:21:13.560
to assess compliant
and security measures.

00:21:13.560 --> 00:21:18.180
You'll also learn how to analyze
irregular access patterns using

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:22.520
advanced tool available in
Audit Premium and PowerShell,

00:21:22.520 --> 00:21:25.700
and how strategic data
management supports

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:29.360
regulatory compliance.

00:21:29.360 --> 00:21:33.380
Microsoft Purview Audit helps
organization understand how

00:21:33.380 --> 00:21:36.740
Microsoft 365 is being used

00:21:36.740 --> 00:21:40.280
by capturing users
and admin activity.

00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:44.930
That visibility supports
security investigation,

00:21:44.930 --> 00:21:49.920
compliance requirement, and
operational transparency.

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:52.560
Audit Standard is
enabled by default,

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:57.720
providing 180-day log
retention that can be accessed

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:01.380
through the portal,
PowerShell, and API,

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:05.190
and supports exporting
results in CSV.

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:08.340
Audit Premium extends
those capabilities

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:13.740
with custom retention policies,
a default one-year retention

00:22:13.740 --> 00:22:19.410
for core services, intelligence
insights into activity patterns,

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:24.150
and higher API bandwidth
for advanced integrations.

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:28.350
Together, these options give
organization flexibility

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:33.000
for day-to-day needs as well
as longer-term investigation

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:34.890
and regulatory requirements.

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:39.570
To put that in context,
Audit Standard is ideal

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:44.220
for general visibility, tracking
basic activities like sign-in,

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:47.670
file modification,
or sharing events.

00:22:47.670 --> 00:22:49.500
Audit Premium, on
the other hand,

00:22:49.500 --> 00:22:53.460
is where you get forensic
depth for longer-term

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:57.690
or higher-risk investigation,
including scenarios

00:22:57.690 --> 00:23:00.870
where you need to know whether
a sensitive message was

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:02.700
actually accessed.

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:07.050
It's also important to remember
that Premium capabilities depend

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:13.560
on licensing, such as Microsoft
365 E5, or E5 Compliance,

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:15.900
or an equivalent add-on.

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:18.990
So you only see these features

00:23:18.990 --> 00:23:22.690
if your environment
is entitled to them.

00:23:23.850 --> 00:23:27.360
Now let's take a look at
configuration and management.

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:31.440
If auditing isn't already
enabled, you can turn it

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:36.120
on through the Purview
portal or by running the

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:39.960
"Set-AdminAuditLogConfig"
cmdlet in PowerShell.

00:23:39.960 --> 00:23:42.420
You also need to
verify licensing.

00:23:42.420 --> 00:23:48.120
Audit Standard is included
in Microsoft 365 E3 and E5,

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:56.130
as well as F1 and F3, and in
Office 365 E1, E3, and E5.

00:23:56.130 --> 00:24:00.060
Audit Premium requires
Microsoft 365 E5,

00:24:00.060 --> 00:24:03.990
E5 Compliance, or
relevant add-on.

00:24:03.990 --> 00:24:08.970
Finally, role determines who
can work with audit logs.

00:24:08.970 --> 00:24:12.420
An audit reader can
search and export log,

00:24:12.420 --> 00:24:15.930
while an audit manager
can search and explore

00:24:15.930 --> 00:24:19.290
but also manage audit settings.

00:24:19.290 --> 00:24:22.230
Even if someone is
a global admin,

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:25.290
they still need the
right role assignment

00:24:25.290 --> 00:24:28.200
in Purview to access logs.

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:34.350
A good investigation habit is to
confirm Audit is enabled early,

00:24:34.350 --> 00:24:40.140
because if logging wasn't
on, then there may be no way

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:43.570
to have an activity
trail to follow.

00:24:44.670 --> 00:24:48.870
Audit Standard provides the
tool needed to search, manage,

00:24:48.870 --> 00:24:53.070
and analyze activity
across Microsoft 365,

00:24:53.070 --> 00:24:55.980
helping organizations
respond to incidents

00:24:55.980 --> 00:24:58.410
and meet compliance
requirements.

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:02.310
You can search audit log using
the Microsoft Purview portal

00:25:02.310 --> 00:25:07.590
or "Vsurge-unifiedauditlog"
cmdlet.

00:25:07.590 --> 00:25:11.820
As you search, you filter
by activity type, user,

00:25:11.820 --> 00:25:16.140
file or site, date
range, workload,

00:25:16.140 --> 00:25:19.740
and you can export
results or access logs

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:23.610
through an API for automation.

00:25:23.610 --> 00:25:28.200
As a result comes in, you
can monitor job progress,

00:25:28.200 --> 00:25:33.150
drill into detail results,
and filter by action, user,

00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:38.070
IP address, record
type, or item detail.

00:25:38.070 --> 00:25:40.770
One practical limitation to keep

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:44.160
in mind is export
scale and standard.

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:47.580
Exports are limited,
so this tier is best

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:52.290
for focused query rather
than large-scale analysis.

00:25:52.290 --> 00:25:55.230
Conceptually, audit logs
help you tell the story

00:25:55.230 --> 00:26:00.150
of an incident, and what you
find depends on what you asked.

00:26:00.150 --> 00:26:02.340
So it's important to
choose filters that align

00:26:02.340 --> 00:26:06.160
to the activity
you expect to see.

00:26:07.410 --> 00:26:11.580
Audit Premium, on the other
hand, provides deeper visibility

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:16.230
into user activity, which helps
you investigate sensitive

00:26:16.230 --> 00:26:19.530
access, detect
suspicious behavior,

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:21.960
and respond to incident.

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:28.710
Classic example is investigating
email access using the mail send

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:30.690
access activity.

00:26:30.690 --> 00:26:35.220
This can show which messages
were accessed, by whom,

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:41.700
and from where within an
activity, bind access logs,

00:26:41.700 --> 00:26:47.070
individual emails used, while
sync access logs bulk downloads,

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:51.540
such as when mailbox is
synchronized to Outlook.

00:26:51.540 --> 00:26:55.800
There are also practical
considerations, like throttling,

00:26:55.800 --> 00:27:00.120
logging pauses after 1,000
buying events per day per

00:27:00.120 --> 00:27:05.430
mailbox, and duplicate entries
are filtered automatically

00:27:05.430 --> 00:27:07.290
to reduce noise.

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:10.620
You can analyze these events
in the Purview portal

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:14.190
or use PowerShell when you
need advanced filtering,

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:18.360
and exporting to CSV helps
to review access type

00:27:18.360 --> 00:27:22.320
and throttling details
into raw audit log.

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:24.930
This is where audit
becomes truly forensic,

00:27:24.930 --> 00:27:26.700
helping answer a question,

00:27:26.700 --> 00:27:30.840
like whether a departing
employee viewed a sensitive

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:36.220
message, not just whether
something was downloaded.

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:42.390
Exporting audit log to CSV
supports deeper analysis

00:27:42.390 --> 00:27:45.150
and helps meet
compliance requirements.

00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:47.490
The process starts
by running a search

00:27:47.490 --> 00:27:51.690
in the Microsoft Purview portal,
and then selecting Excel

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:57.540
to download up to 50,000
audit records as a CSV file.

00:27:57.540 --> 00:28:02.700
For analysis, you can open
the CSV in Excel using Data,

00:28:02.700 --> 00:28:07.950
then from Text/CSV, and
use Power Query Editor

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:11.880
to transform the audit
data columns from JSON

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:14.580
into readable columns.

00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:17.580
From there, you expand the
properties you care about,

00:28:17.580 --> 00:28:22.560
such as IP address,
access type, item detail,

00:28:22.560 --> 00:28:25.410
so you can focus on
what's relevant.

00:28:25.410 --> 00:28:29.460
This is especially useful when
you need to correlate events

00:28:29.460 --> 00:28:33.270
across services or
identify anomalies

00:28:33.270 --> 00:28:38.710
such as unusual access
during nonbusiness hours.

00:28:39.750 --> 00:28:43.830
Audit retention is a major
part of meeting regulatory

00:28:43.830 --> 00:28:47.010
and investigation requirements.

00:28:47.010 --> 00:28:51.270
With Audit Premium, you get
custom retention policies

00:28:51.270 --> 00:28:55.500
that control how long
audit data is kept.

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:59.790
By default Premium provides a
one-year retention for Exchange,

00:28:59.790 --> 00:29:04.080
SharePoint, OneDrive,
and Microsoft Entra ID.

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:07.320
That default policy
can't be changed,

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:12.150
but custom policies get
overwritten for specific needs.

00:29:12.150 --> 00:29:15.090
With custom retention
policies you can create

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:20.580
up to 50 policies scoped to
specific users, record types,

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:25.560
or activities, and retain data
from seven days all the way

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:29.700
up to 10 years, although a
10-year retention requires an

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:31.680
add-on license.

00:29:31.680 --> 00:29:36.840
One important operational detail
is that retention is determined

00:29:36.840 --> 00:29:39.330
at the time data is logged.

00:29:39.330 --> 00:29:44.670
So changes apply only to
new data going forward.

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:49.170
From a risk perspective, longer
retention increases resilience

00:29:49.170 --> 00:29:52.530
from slow-developing
incidents and helps

00:29:52.530 --> 00:29:55.560
in regulated industries
where investigations

00:29:55.560 --> 00:30:00.930
or audits may look back years.

00:30:00.930 --> 00:30:03.960
Let's recap what we've
covered in this module.

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:05.250
We examined the difference

00:30:05.250 --> 00:30:08.100
between Microsoft
Purview Audit Standard

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:11.730
and Microsoft Purview Audit
Premium, how to search

00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:15.780
for audited activities using
Microsoft Purview Audit,

00:30:15.780 --> 00:30:20.820
and how to implement audit
log searching in practice.

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:24.360
We also discussed how audit log
searches can help investigate

00:30:24.360 --> 00:30:28.590
common support issues and
how Audit Premium builds

00:30:28.590 --> 00:30:31.470
on the capability
of Audit Standard

00:30:31.470 --> 00:30:34.530
to enable deeper investigation.

00:30:34.530 --> 00:30:36.270
Finally, we covered how

00:30:36.270 --> 00:30:39.450
to create audit log
prevention policies,

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:44.010
and how auditing supports
forensic investigation

00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:47.550
of compromised user accounts.

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:53.740
Now, let's see Microsoft Purview
in action in the short demo.

00:30:55.350 --> 00:30:59.550
Let's learn how to start
an audit in Purview.

00:30:59.550 --> 00:31:02.340
We're currently in
Microsoft Defender,

00:31:02.340 --> 00:31:04.950
but let's access
Purview from here.

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:10.500
From the Defender bar, let's
move down to "More Resources".

00:31:10.500 --> 00:31:13.950
This gives us access to
link to additional portals,

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:16.980
including the Microsoft
Purview portal.

00:31:16.980 --> 00:31:18.940
Let's open it.

00:31:19.980 --> 00:31:22.020
Now, when we open
it, it will tell us

00:31:22.020 --> 00:31:24.870
that the old compliance
portal is retired.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:28.150
Let's manually switch
to the new portal.

00:31:31.860 --> 00:31:36.480
And here we are in the
Microsoft Purview portal.

00:31:36.480 --> 00:31:39.870
The only thing we're going to
do over here is explore how

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:43.020
to start an audit
and basic search.

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:47.410
Let's go into
"Solutions," "Audit."

00:31:50.040 --> 00:31:51.450
There's our button,

00:31:51.450 --> 00:31:56.280
"Start recording users
and admit activity."

00:31:56.280 --> 00:31:57.690
We'll start that.

00:31:57.690 --> 00:32:01.260
It will take a moment or
two to start the audit,

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:03.390
and from that point forward,

00:32:03.390 --> 00:32:06.150
after we've captured
enough information,

00:32:06.150 --> 00:32:12.700
we can start querying and
create cases as necessary.

00:32:15.030 --> 00:32:18.720
Let's wrap up with a quick
Learning Path recap.

00:32:18.720 --> 00:32:21.810
We've covered how Microsoft
Purview provides compliance

00:32:21.810 --> 00:32:24.150
solutions to mitigate threats.

00:32:24.150 --> 00:32:27.120
We discussed "Content
Search" as a key feature

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:31.350
within Microsoft Purview
eDiscovery, and also looked

00:32:31.350 --> 00:32:33.900
at Microsoft Purview
Audit Solution,

00:32:33.900 --> 00:32:37.650
including both Audit
Standard and Audit Premium.

00:32:37.650 --> 00:32:40.890
You saw that Audit Standard
is enabled by default

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:43.830
and provides the ability
to log and search

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:47.970
for audited activities, and
that Audit Premium builds

00:32:47.970 --> 00:32:52.800
on that foundation by providing
advanced auditing functionality

00:32:52.800 --> 00:32:58.120
for deeper investigations and
longer-term requirements.

00:32:59.460 --> 00:33:01.560
And this completes our session.

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:04.920
There are many ways to continue
your learning journey.

00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:08.670
We encourage you to watch other
